      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so i am stuck on an iam policy issue. i have a lambda that needs to write logs to an s3 bucket but i keep getting access denied errors in the cloudwatch logs even though i think the policy is right. can you check this json for me?\n\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"s3:PutObject\",\n        \"s3:GetBucketLocation\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::my-app-logs-bucket/*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The IAM policy you've posted does allow the Lambda function to put objects in the S3 bucket, which is required for log writing. However, make sure that your Lambda function has the necessary permissions to write logs to CloudWatch as well as the proper IAM roles assigned to it. You might need to add the following statement to your IAM policy to grant CloudWatch log writing permissions:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Action\": \"logs:CreateLogStream\",\n  \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n  \"Resource\": \"*\"\n}\n```\n\nEnsure your Lambda execution role has sufficient permissions and then check if your Lambda function needs any additional S3 bucket policies to resolve the access denied errors.",
